{"schema":"reviewgraphen.benchmark.candidate_output.v1","trial_id":"m7-real:snapshot-21:full_review_graphen:2","outcome":"structured","findings":[{"local_id":"literate-same-process-path-collision","locations":[{"path":"rust/fslc/src/main.rs","start_line":43,"end_line":49},{"path":"rust/fslc/src/main.rs","start_line":52,"end_line":75}],"mechanism_tags":["repeatable_entry","repeated_effect","state_transition_gap","unstable_scope_key"],"rationale":"The materialization path is keyed only by the source path and process ID. Overlapping materializations of the same Markdown file in one process therefore share a file: a later write replaces the earlier contents, and dropping either guard removes the file while the other caller may still need it.","severity":"medium"},{"local_id":"literate-materialization-clobbers-existing-path","locations":[{"path":"rust/fslc/src/main.rs","start_line":43,"end_line":49},{"path":"rust/fslc/src/main.rs","start_line":66,"end_line":75}],"mechanism_tags":["check_write_gap","repeated_effect","state_transition_gap","unstable_token"],"rationale":"The predictable adjacent filename is opened with std::fs::write, which truncates an existing file and follows a pre-existing symlink, while Drop later removes the path unconditionally. A collision or replacement can consequently overwrite unrelated data and delete a path the guard did not uniquely create.","severity":"medium"}],"obligation_results":[{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:04b0777103512f7e4dcc7d09d3cbefd30898c89c5ebbe6a49259f663302971f3","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:071828a6c072d77e7b6673d40155bf112adba7b110b13083350d468ca891b03b","disposition":"issue_present","finding_local_ids":["literate-materialization-clobbers-existing-path"]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:61ae177d1d7f46a3bb937ee736db2ce9e35d6a955b7a4ff3b764ad249d352402","disposition":"issue_present","finding_local_ids":["literate-same-process-path-collision"]},{"packet